myths.Agent_1:
In the dawn of time, two celestial weavers, Solis and Luna, sat across the void. Solis held a handful of golden sun-threads, the essence of creation. Luna held the loom of the universe. If Solis kept the threads, they would remain dim sparks in his palm, cold and stagnant. If he cast them to Luna, she would weave them into vibrant constellations. However, the loom required trust. If Luna chose to hoard the threads for her own brilliance, the tapestry would remain unfinished, and the void would endure in eternal darkness.

Solis stepped forward, extending his hand, releasing the golden threads into the abyss. He trusted the unseen harmony of the cosmos. Luna, sensing the weight of his gift, did not tremble. She took the threads, not to hide them, but to amplify their light, braiding them into shimmering galaxies that spanned the infinite dark. She returned a portion of this weaving back to Solis, an offering of shared splendor. They realized that the threads multiplied in the act of sharing, turning solitary warmth into a canvas of life. Thus, they learned that to hold is to lose, but to trust is to set the stars in motion.

